    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d161">

                <pb facs="0160" n="154" sameAs="frus:frus1942v02/pg154"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.00/2699: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Leahy</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Vichy</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-03-26T15:00:00+02:00">March 26, 1942—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 26—2:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>451. For the Acting Secretary. The official mentioned in our 690, June 14, 10
                    a.m., 1941<anchor n="36" xml:id="d161-f1"/> in conversation with a member of
                    this Embassy yesterday stated that since a previous conversation (see our 417,
                    March 19, 5 p.m.<anchor n="36" xml:id="d161-f2"/>) German pressure on Vichy has
                    become much more intense.</p>
                <p>This pressure while not yet in ultimatum form is being applied through
                        Benoist-Méchin,<anchor n="37" xml:id="d161-f3"/> Abetz<anchor n="38"
                        xml:id="d161-f4"/> and de Brinon<anchor n="39" xml:id="d161-f5"/> and is
                    directed toward the establishment of a definitely collaborationist government
                    prior to the spring offensive in Russia.</p>
                <p>He said that Germany is pressing for the return of Laval<anchor n="40"
                        xml:id="d161-f6"/> which is extremely displeasing to the Marshal<anchor
                        n="41" xml:id="d161-f7"/> who is, however, very friendly with René de
                        Chambrun.<anchor n="42" xml:id="d161-f8"/>
                </p>
                <p>Our informant fears that a definitely pro-collaborationist government will in the
                    near future be forced upon the Marshal. He said also <pb facs="0161" n="155"
                        xml:id="pg155"/> that Xavier Vallat, Commissioner for Jewish Affairs, and
                    Xucheu [<hi rendition="#italic">Pucheu?</hi>]<anchor n="43" xml:id="d161-f9"/>
                    have now been refused permission to travel between the occupied and unoccupied
                    zones and that a decree putting an early end to the Riom trial will shortly be
                    issued following German pressure.</p>
                <p>The individual mentioned in our 1490, December 3, 2 p.m., 1941<anchor n="44"
                        xml:id="d161-f10"/> told us today that Germany will no longer negotiate with
                    the present Government, that a change will be made at a time chosen by Germany
                    and that Laval will be returned to the Cabinet, the matter having already been
                    discussed with the Marshal by René de Chambrun.</p>
                <p>We are told by a reliable informant who obtained his information from Laval that
                    the Marshal and Laval had a secret meeting this morning in the country near
                    Vichy in which the question of Laval’s return to the Cabinet was discussed.</p>
                <p>Arrangements have been made for Darlan to confer with Laval Saturday at
                    Châteldon.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Leahy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d161-f1" n="36" xml:id="d161-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d161-f2" n="36" xml:id="d161-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d161-f3" n="37" xml:id="d161-f3-content">
                <p>Jacques Benoist-Méchin, French Secretary of State to the President of the Council
                    of Ministers.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d161-f4" n="38" xml:id="d161-f4-content">
                <p>Otto Abetz, German Ambassador on special mission in Paris.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d161-f5" n="39" xml:id="d161-f5-content">
                <p>Fernand de Brinon, General Delegate of French Government for Occupied
                    Territories.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d161-f6" n="40" xml:id="d161-f6-content">
                <p>Pierre Laval, named Chief of French Government April 18, 1942.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d161-f7" n="41" xml:id="d161-f7-content">
                <p>Marshal Pétain, French Chief of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d161-f8" n="42" xml:id="d161-f8-content">
                <p>Son-in-law of Pierre Laval.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d161-f9" n="43" xml:id="d161-f9-content">
                <p>Pierre Pucheu, French Minister of Interior.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d161-f10" n="44" xml:id="d161-f10-content">
                <p>Not printed; the individual is identified only as a career diplomat and member of
                    Marshal Pétain’s “entourage” (851.00/2529).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
